Subject: [PATCH] Data: verify all six coolant specs, fix two wrong chemistries

Researched ahead of writing coolant flush guides. The guides did not get
written -- see below -- but the verification pass found two vehicles
listing the wrong coolant, which is the field where an error actually
destroys an engine rather than just annoying someone.

ESCAPE -- WRONG CHEMISTRY. Listed Motorcraft Orange (Gold). The 2020+
Escape's 1.5L three-cylinder takes Motorcraft YELLOW, WSS-M97B57-A2,
confirmed against three independent sources. The trap here is worth
recording: a Ford owner-manual page headed "Capacities and Specifications
- 1.5L EcoBoost" does list Orange, but it describes the 2017-2019
Escape's 1.5L FOUR-cylinder. Same model name, same displacement,
different engine, different coolant. Capacity also corrected, 6.9 -> 7.6 qt.

F-150 -- CONFLATED TWO SPECS. Listed "Motorcraft Orange (Gold)", as
though those were one product. Orange is WSS-M97B44-D; Gold is VC-7 /
WSS-M97B51-A1, a separate specification. Now names Orange and its spec
number, plus the note that Ford has approved Motorcraft Yellow as the
service replacement for anything factory-filled with Orange and is
phasing Orange out -- while explicitly warning Yellow is not a universal
substitute and must not go into a vehicle factory-filled with any other
Motorcraft coolant, Gold included.

JEEP -- WRONG FORMULA AND MISLEADING COLOR. Listed "Mopar OAT (orange),
5-year/100k". The 2014 owner's manual names the 10 Year/150,000 Mile OAT
formula. Orange is the older HOAT chemistry Chrysler used earlier in this
same generation, and 2013-2014 is the changeover window, so a color match
at the parts counter is exactly how HOAT ends up in an OAT system. Now
tells the owner to confirm against the underhood label; the factory color
could not be verified from primary literature and is no longer claimed.

CIVIC and the GM TRUCKS -- verified correct, unchanged. Honda Type 2 blue
and Dex-Cool respectively. The Civic gained a note that Type 2 is
phosphate-based and silicate-free, so a European formula is not a safe
substitute.

WHY NO COOLANT GUIDES SHIPPED

Chemistry verified cleanly; procedure did not. Radiator drain method and
the bleed/burp procedure came back UNVERIFIED for five of six vehicles --
that information lives in factory service manuals rather than owner's
manuals, and the open web kept returning the wrong generation. Bleed is
the step DIYers skip and the reason a home coolant change ends in an
overheat, so a guide that guesses at it is worse than no guide. Not
written until sourced.

Verified: next build compiles clean; all 28 guides still pass every
required check; eslint unchanged at the same 4 pre-existing problems.

